.TH XDLFCN 3 "2026" "Extern Library" "Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
xdlfcn - cross-platform dynamic library loading and symbol lookup
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include "xdlfcn.h"
.PP
.BI "char *xdllibpath(const char *" dir ", const char *" name ");"
.BI "wchar_t *xwdllibpath(const wchar_t *" dir ", const wchar_t *" name ");"
.BI "const char *xcdllibpath(const char *" dir ", const char *" name ");"
.BI "const wchar_t *xcwdllibpath(const wchar_t *" dir ", const wchar_t *" name ");"
.PP
.B "typedef struct {"
.B "    const char *dli_fname;"
.B "    void *dli_fbase;"
.B "    const char *dli_sname;"
.B "    void *dli_saddr;"
.B "} XDl_info;"
.PP
.BI "int xdladdr(const void *" addr ", XDl_info *" info ");"
.PP
.BI "xhandle_t xdlopen(const char *" file ", int " mode ");"
.BI "xhandle_t xwdlopen(const wchar_t *" file ", int " mode ");"
.BI "xproc_t xdlsym(xhandle_t " handle ", const char *" name ");"
.BI "xproc_t xwdlsym(xhandle_t " handle ", const wchar_t *" name ");"
.B "char *xdlerror(void);"
.B "wchar_t *xwdlerror(void);"
.BI "int xdlclose(xhandle_t " handle ");"
.PP
.BI "FARPROC GetProcAddressA(HMODULE " hModule ", LPCSTR " lpProcName ");"
.BI "FARPROC GetProcAddressW(HMODULE " hModule ", LPCWSTR " lpProcName ");"
.PP
On non-Windows systems:
.PP
.BI "HMODULE GetModuleHandleA(LPCSTR " lpModuleName ");"
.BI "HMODULE GetModuleHandleW(LPCWSTR " lpModuleName ");"
.BI "HMODULE LoadLibraryA(LPCSTR " lpLibFileName ");"
.BI "HMODULE LoadLibraryW(LPCWSTR " lpLibFileName ");"
.BI "FARPROC GetProcAddress(HMODULE " hModule ", LPCSTR " lpProcName ");"
.BI "WINBOOL FreeLibrary(HMODULE " hLibModule ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B xdlfcn.h
header provides a cross-platform interface for constructing library paths, loading and unloading dynamic libraries, looking up exported symbols, reporting loader errors, and obtaining information about the loaded object associated with an address.
.PP
On POSIX systems, the interface uses the standard dynamic-loading facilities declared in
.BR <dlfcn.h> ,
including
.BR dlopen (3),
.BR dlsym (3),
.BR dlerror (3),
and
.BR dlclose (3).
On Windows, it uses the Windows dynamic-loader APIs.
.PP
The header includes
.BR "xtypes.h"
and
.BR "xstddef.h" ,
which provide project-specific types and declarations used by the interface.
.SH LOADER FLAGS
The
.B XRTLD_*
macros define portable names for dynamic-loader flags and symbol-lookup controls. Their definitions and runtime semantics depend on the platform.
.TP
.B XRTLD_LAZY
Request lazy symbol resolution where supported. On POSIX systems, this maps to
.BR RTLD_LAZY .
Windows does not provide an equivalent flag with distinct lazy-binding semantics.
.TP
.B XRTLD_NOW
Request symbol resolution when loading the library where supported. On POSIX systems, this maps to
.BR RTLD_NOW .
Windows does not provide an equivalent flag with distinct immediate-binding semantics.
.TP
.B XRTLD_BINDING_MASK
Mask for binding-related flags. On POSIX systems, this maps to
.BR RTLD_BINDING_MASK .
.TP
.B XRTLD_NOLOAD
Request a handle to an already loaded library without loading a new one where supported. On POSIX systems, this maps to
.B RTLD_NOLOAD
when available; otherwise, the header defines the fallback value
.BR 0x00004 .
On Windows, the header also defines this value as a project-specific flag.
.TP
.B XRTLD_DEEPBIND
Request deep symbol binding where supported. On POSIX systems, this maps to
.B RTLD_DEEPBIND
when available; otherwise, the header defines the fallback value
.BR 0x00008 .
Windows has no direct equivalent.
.TP
.B XRTLD_NODELETE
Request that a library remain mapped after unloading where supported. On POSIX systems, this maps to
.B RTLD_NODELETE
when available; otherwise, the header defines the fallback value
.BR 0x00010 .
.TP
.B XRTLD_LOCAL
Keep the loaded library's symbols local where supported. On POSIX systems, this maps to
.BR RTLD_LOCAL .
The Windows definition is zero.
.TP
.B XRTLD_GLOBAL
Make the loaded library's symbols globally available where supported. On POSIX systems, this maps to
.BR RTLD_GLOBAL .
The Windows definition is
.BR 0x00100 .
.TP
.B XRTLD_FIRST
Request first-level symbol lookup where supported. The header defines this as zero on Windows and maps it to
.B RTLD_FIRST
on Apple platforms when available.
.TP
.B XRTLD_NEXT
Special lookup handle for finding the next definition of a symbol after the current object where supported. The header maps this to
.B RTLD_NEXT
when available and otherwise defines a fallback handle value.
.TP
.B XRTLD_DEFAULT
Special lookup handle for searching the default symbol scope where supported. The header maps this to
.B RTLD_DEFAULT
when available and otherwise defines a platform-specific fallback handle.
.TP
.B XRTLD_SELF
Apple-specific special lookup handle for the current image and its dependencies. Defined only when
.B **APPLE**
is set.
.TP
.B XRTLD_MAIN_ONLY
Apple-specific special lookup handle for the main executable only. Defined only when
.B **APPLE**
is set.
.PP
Fallback constants ensure that macros can be referenced at compile time; they do not guarantee that the underlying platform implements the corresponding runtime behavior. Consult the implementation before relying on a flag whose native definition is unavailable.
.SH LIBRARY PATH UTILITIES
.SS xdllibpath()
.nf
.BI "char *xdllibpath(const char *" dir ", const char *" name ");"
.fi
Constructs a library path from a directory and a library name using narrow-character strings.
.PP
Returns a mutable character pointer. The header does not specify the allocation method, ownership rules, path separator behavior, or the lifetime of the returned string.
.SS xwdllibpath()
.nf
.BI "wchar_t *xwdllibpath(const wchar_t *" dir ", const wchar_t *" name ");"
.fi
Wide-character counterpart of
.BR xdllibpath ().
Constructs a library path using wide-character strings.
.PP
Returns a mutable wide-character pointer. Allocation and ownership rules depend on the implementation.
.SS xcdllibpath()
.nf
.BI "const char *xcdllibpath(const char *" dir ", const char *" name ");"
.fi
Narrow-character library-path utility returning a pointer to constant character data.
.PP
The header does not specify whether the result uses static, cached, or dynamically allocated storage. Consult the implementation for the result's lifetime and thread-safety guarantees.
.SS xcwdllibpath()
.nf
.BI "const wchar_t *xcwdllibpath(const wchar_t *" dir ", const wchar_t *" name ");"
.fi
Wide-character counterpart of
.BR xcdllibpath ().
Returns a pointer to constant wide-character data. The storage and lifetime of the result depend on the implementation.
.SH ADDRESS INFORMATION
.SS xdladdr()
.nf
.BI "int xdladdr(const void *" addr ", XDl_info *" info ");"
.fi
Attempts to retrieve information about the loaded object and nearest symbol associated with
.IR addr .
The result is written to the structure pointed to by
.IR info .
.PP
The
.B XDl_info
structure contains the following members:
.TP
.B dli_fname
File name of the object defining the address.
.TP
.B dli_fbase
Load or base address of that object.
.TP
.B dli_sname
Name of the nearest symbol associated with the address, when available.
.TP
.B dli_saddr
Address associated with that symbol, when available.
.PP
The header does not specify the function's success and failure return values or guarantee that symbol information is available on every platform. Refer to the implementation for those details.
.SH LOADING AND UNLOADING
.SS xdlopen()
.nf
.BI "xhandle_t xdlopen(const char *" file ", int " mode ");"
.fi
Loads a dynamic library specified by a narrow-character filename.
.PP
On POSIX systems, the function uses
.BR dlopen (3).
The
.I mode
argument accepts the applicable
.B XRTLD_*
flags.
On Windows, the function uses the native library loader. Flags without native Windows equivalents may have no distinct effect or may be handled specially by the implementation.
.PP
Returns an
.B xhandle_t
handle on success or
.B NULL
on failure.
.SS xwdlopen()
.nf
.BI "xhandle_t xwdlopen(const wchar_t *" file ", int " mode ");"
.fi
Wide-character counterpart of
.BR xdlopen ().
.PP
On Windows, the function uses wide-character native loader facilities. On POSIX systems, the filename is converted to a multibyte representation using
.BR xwcstombs ()
before being passed to
.BR dlopen (3).
.PP
Returns an
.B xhandle_t
handle on success or
.B NULL
on failure.
.SS xdlclose()
.nf
.BI "int xdlclose(xhandle_t " handle ");"
.fi
Releases a reference to a previously loaded dynamic library.
.PP
On POSIX systems, the function uses
.BR dlclose (3).
On Windows, it uses the native library-unloading API.
.PP
Returns an integer status. The precise failure value and error-reporting behavior should be verified against the implementation.
.SH SYMBOL LOOKUP
.SS xdlsym()
.nf
.BI "xproc_t xdlsym(xhandle_t " handle ", const char *" name ");"
.fi
Looks up an exported symbol using a narrow-character name.
.PP
On POSIX systems, the function uses
.BR dlsym (3).
On Windows, it uses
.BR GetProcAddress ().
The
.I handle
argument identifies a library or a supported special lookup scope, and
.I name
specifies the symbol.
.PP
Returns a
.B xproc_t
symbol address or a failure value as defined by the implementation.
.SS xwdlsym()
.nf
.BI "xproc_t xwdlsym(xhandle_t " handle ", const wchar_t *" name ");"
.fi
Wide-character counterpart of
.BR xdlsym ().
The symbol name is converted to the representation required by the underlying lookup API.
.PP
On Windows, symbol lookup ultimately uses
.BR GetProcAddress ().
On POSIX systems, the converted name is passed to
.BR dlsym (3).
.PP
Returns a
.B xproc_t
symbol address or a failure value as defined by the implementation.
.SH ERROR REPORTING
.SS xdlerror()
.nf
.B "char *xdlerror(void);"
.fi
Returns a narrow-character description of a dynamic-loader error.
.PP
On POSIX systems, the function uses
.BR dlerror (3).
POSIX error handling has its own loader error state; callers should check the result of the preceding operation and retrieve the error when appropriate.
.PP
On Windows, the function may obtain an error code through
.BR GetLastError ()
and format a human-readable message using
.BR FormatMessageA ().
The header does not specify buffer lifetime or whether retrieving the message consumes or resets the underlying error state.
.SS xwdlerror()
.nf
.B "wchar_t *xwdlerror(void);"
.fi
Wide-character counterpart of
.BR xdlerror ().
.PP
On Windows, the function may use
.BR FormatMessageW ()
to produce a wide-character error message. On POSIX systems, it may convert the result of
.BR dlerror (3)
using
.BR xmbstowcs ().
.PP
The header does not specify the lifetime of the returned pointer or the behavior when no error is available.
.SH WINDOWS-COMPATIBLE WRAPPERS
.SS GetProcAddressA()
.nf
.BI "FARPROC GetProcAddressA(HMODULE " hModule ", LPCSTR " lpProcName ");"
.fi
Looks up an exported symbol using a narrow-character name. This function provides a Windows-style narrow-character symbol-lookup interface.
.SS GetProcAddressW()
.nf
.BI "FARPROC GetProcAddressW(HMODULE " hModule ", LPCWSTR " lpProcName ");"
.fi
Wide-character symbol-lookup interface. The native Windows
.B GetProcAddress()
API accepts narrow-character symbol names, so an implementation must convert the supplied name before performing the lookup.
.PP
On non-Windows systems, these functions provide compatibility interfaces implemented by the library.
.SH POSIX COMPATIBILITY WRAPPERS
On non-Windows systems, the header declares Windows-style library-management interfaces.
.SS GetModuleHandleA() and GetModuleHandleW()
.nf
.BI "HMODULE GetModuleHandleA(LPCSTR " lpModuleName ");"
.BI "HMODULE GetModuleHandleW(LPCWSTR " lpModuleName ");"
.fi
Provide narrow-character and wide-character interfaces for obtaining a module handle. The compatibility implementation may use the no-load lookup mechanism where supported.
.SS LoadLibraryA() and LoadLibraryW()
.nf
.BI "HMODULE LoadLibraryA(LPCSTR " lpLibFileName ");"
.BI "HMODULE LoadLibraryW(LPCWSTR " lpLibFileName ");"
.fi
Load a library using narrow-character and wide-character filenames, respectively. The compatibility implementation may use
.B XRTLD_LAZY
as its default loading mode.
.SS GetProcAddress()
.nf
.BI "FARPROC GetProcAddress(HMODULE " hModule ", LPCSTR " lpProcName ");"
.fi
Looks up an exported symbol using a narrow-character name.
.SS FreeLibrary()
.nf
.BI "WINBOOL FreeLibrary(HMODULE " hLibModule ");"
.fi
Releases a library handle and returns a
.B WINBOOL
result. Its implementation corresponds to the platform's dynamic-library unloading mechanism.
.PP
The header defines the generic
.B GetModuleHandle
and
.B LoadLibrary
names using
.BR __LIBXC_NAME_AW ,
which selects the appropriate narrow- or wide-character API according to the project's configuration.
.SH IMPLEMENTATION CONSTANTS
The header defines the following constants:
.TP
.B XDLFCN_ERROR_BUFFER
Defined as 512. Intended as an error-buffer size constant.
.TP
.B XDLFCN_PATH_BUFFER
Defined as
.BR XPATH_MAX .
Intended as a path-buffer size constant.
.TP
.B XDLFCN_MAX_SYM_NAME
Defined as 2000. Intended as a symbol-name length constant.
.PP
These constants are declared by the header. The header alone does not establish whether they impose hard limits on public functions.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include "xdlfcn.h"

int main(void)
{
xhandle_t handle;
xproc_t symbol;
char *error;

```
handle = xdlopen("libexample.so", XRTLD_NOW);
if (handle == NULL) {
    error = xdlerror();
    if (error != NULL)
        fprintf(stderr, "xdlopen: %s\\n", error);
    return 1;
}

symbol = xdlsym(handle, "example_function");
if (symbol == NULL) {
    error = xdlerror();
    if (error != NULL)
        fprintf(stderr, "xdlsym: %s\\n", error);
    xdlclose(handle);
    return 1;
}

/*
 * Use the symbol according to its known function type.
 * Converting FARPROC to a function pointer is platform-dependent.
 */

xdlclose(handle);
return 0;
```

}
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
The header does not specify the thread-safety guarantees of the public functions.
.PP
Error-state handling, internal buffers, and the lifetime of returned path or error strings depend on the implementation and underlying platform.
.PP
A library must not be unloaded while another thread may execute code from it or access symbols whose validity depends on it remaining loaded.
.SH PORTABILITY NOTES
The interface uses project-defined types and calling-convention macros, including
.BR HMODULE ,
.BR FARPROC ,
.BR WINBOOL ,
.BR XSTDAPI ,
and
.BR XCALLAPI .
Programs must include the required project headers and link against the appropriate library implementation.
.PP
The POSIX loader flags and Windows loader flags do not have identical semantics. In particular, fallback macro definitions make some names available at compile time without guaranteeing equivalent runtime behavior.
.PP
The filename
.B libexample.so
in the example is typical of many Unix-like systems. Windows library filenames and extensions differ; applications should supply a filename appropriate for their target platform.
